# What a 1% fee really costs
subtitle: "Watch a 1% yearly fund fee quietly eat about two fifths of fifty years of savings, then move the fee slider yourself."

## What a 1% fee really costs
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - '0'
      - '10'
      - '20'
      - '30'
      - '40'
      - '50'
    series:
      - {name: No fee, values: '{calc.gross}'}
      - {name: With fee, values: '{calc.frames}'}
    stage: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 3200
    value_labels: false
    x_label: Years
    y_label: Value of $100
    box:
      x: 420
      'y': 190
      w: 540
      h: 390

    ```brython calc x 200 y 170 width 360 font_size 26 show code controls ["fee"] pane controls hidden true
    years = [0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
    fee = Slider(0, 3, step=0.25, default=1, label="Yearly fee, %")
    gross = [round(100 * 1.07 ** y) for y in years]
    net = [round(100 * (1.07 * (1 - fee / 100)) ** y) for y in years]
    frames = [[0] * 6, net]
    ```

    card title "What a 1% fee really costs"

How much can a one percent yearly fee cost? Follow a hundred dollars growing at seven percent a year for fifty years.
    draw axes

Without a fee, the whole pot earns seven percent each year, and it roughly doubles every decade.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4 bar_5 bar_6 parallel true

After fifty years: two thousand nine hundred and forty-six dollars.
    text cue_gross "No fee: $2,946" #b5482e x 210 y 540 font_size 34

Now a fund keeps one percent of the savings every year. The fee is this slider.
    draw calc calc_ctls

Each year the pot grows seven percent, then gives up one percent of itself. Same money, same markets.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

After fifty years: one thousand seven hundred and eighty-two.
    text cue_net "1% fee: $1,782" #2f6b8f x 210 y 480 font_size 34

So a fee of one percent a year took about two fifths of the final pot.
    text cue_loss "−40%" #3a3530 x 210 y 405 font_size 44

The fee compounds too: every dollar it takes early stops earning for all the years that follow.
    point at.ref bar_2 gesture circle

    erase cue_net cue_loss

Slide the fee up to two percent, and the last bar sinks to just over a third of the fee-free pot.
    run code calc values.fee.from 1 values.fee.to 2 values.fee.steps 5

One thousand and seventy-three dollars.
    text cue_two "2% fee: $1,073" #2f6b8f x 210 y 480 font_size 34

    erase cue_two

This is why John Bogle started the first index fund for ordinary savers in 1976: to keep fees close to nothing.
    portrait portrait_john_c_bogle x 300 y 360 width 120 of "John C. Bogle" cameo true

    erase portrait_john_c_bogle

Now set the fee your own fund charges, and see what it costs by year fifty.
    explore code calc

    quiz "A fund charges 1% a year. Over 50 years at 7%, about how much of the final pot does the fee take?"
        right: About forty percent: every dollar the fee takes stops compounding, so the loss grows with the savings.
        * About 1 %
        * About 10 %
        + About 40 %
---
